\begin{abstract}
We present the full mathematical reduction of Persian as a canonical example, then generalize to any natural language. The reduction consists of: a finite‑dimensional feature space \(\R^n\) into which phonemes are embedded; a free or presented monoid of phonological strings; a stress function; a reduction function; a context‑free grammar with \(\lambda\)-semantics; a prosody function; a visual form (ribbon); symmetry groups; fractal dimension; and a computational implementation. We then prove the \textbf{Ribbon Representation Theorem}: every utterance of every language is isomorphic to a piecewise linear path (ribbon) in \(\R^n\), carrying a syntax tree and a pitch contour. All mathematics is explicit; proofs are constructive. The work ends with Q.E.D.
\end{abstract}

\tableofcontents

\section{Introduction}

The goal is to reduce any natural language to a finite set of mathematical objects. We first demonstrate the method on Persian, then extract the universal template, and finally prove that every language yields a ribbon structure.

\section{Reduction of Persian: A Complete Example}

\subsection{Vowel Feature Space}

\begin{definition}[Persian Vowels]
The Persian vowel inventory:
\[
\V = \{a,\ e,\ o,\ \hat{a},\ i,\ u\}
\]
Phonetic values: \(a = /\ae/\), \(e = /e/\), \(o = /o/\), \(\hat{a} = /\ɒ/\), \(i = /i/\), \(u = /u/\).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Feature Map]
A map \(f: \V \to \R^3\) using height \(h\), backness \(b\), roundness \(r\):
\[
\begin{array}{c|ccc}
v & h & b & r \\ \hline
a & -1 & 1 & -1\\
e & 0 & 1 & -1\\
o & 0 & -1 & 1\\
\hat{a} & -1 & -1 & -1\\
i & 1 & 1 & -1\\
u & 1 & -1 & 1
\end{array}
\]
\end{definition}

\subsection{Phonological Monoid}

\begin{definition}[Vowel Sequence Monoid]
The set of all finite vowel sequences \(\V^*\) forms a free monoid with concatenation \(\cdot\) and identity \(\epsilon\). This is \textbf{free} because Persian imposes no vowel‑harmony constraints.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Stress Function}

\begin{definition}[Heavy Syllable]
A vowel is \textbf{heavy} iff it is long: \(\hat{a}, i, u\).
For a word with vowel sequence \(\pi = v_1\cdots v_n\), stress falls on the last heavy syllable:
\[
\stress(\pi) = \max\{j \mid \text{heavy}(v_j)=1\}
\]
if the set is non‑empty; otherwise \(\stress(\pi)=n\) (final syllable).
\end{definition}

\subsection{Reduction (Vowel Reduction)}

\begin{definition}[Reduction Rule]
In fast speech, unstressed short vowels \(a, e, o\) reduce to \([e]\) (schwa). Let \(\reduce: \V^* \to \V^*\) replace each unstressed short vowel by the vowel whose feature vector is \((0,1,-1)\).
\end{definition}

\subsection{Syntax and Semantics}

We present a categorial grammar (simplified). Basic types: \(e\) (entity), \(t\) (truth value), \(s\) (world/time). Sample lexicon:

\[
\begin{array}{lll}
\text{man} & : e & \text{Speaker}\\
\text{ketâb} & : e\to t & \lambda x.\,\text{book}(x)\\
\text{mi-rav-} & : e\to(s\to t) & \lambda x\lambda w\lambda t.[t\subseteq now\land go(w)(t)(x)]\\
\text{na-} & : (e\to(s\to t))\to(e\to(s\to t)) & \lambda V\lambda x.\neg V(x)\\
\text{-am} & : (e\to(s\to t))\to(s\to t) & \lambda V.V(\text{Speaker})
\end{array}
\]

Syntactic rule for SOV: \(S \to NP\; VP\) with semantics \(VP(NP)\).  
Ezafe construction: \(NP \to N\; A\) with semantics \(\lambda x[N(x)\land A(N)(x)]\).

\subsection{Prosody}

Declarative pitch contour (falling after stress):
\[
\pitch_{decl}(t) = 
\begin{cases}
P_{max} - \alpha t, & 0\le t \le t_{stress},\\
P_{max} - \alpha t_{stress} - \beta(t-t_{stress}), & t>t_{stress},
\end{cases}
\]
with \(\beta>\alpha>0\).

\subsection{Visual Form}

The vowel sequence becomes a piecewise linear path (ribbon) through the 6 points in \(\R^3\). Stress is shown as brighter/larger nodes, reduction as fading. The syntax tree is overlaid as a branching structure above the ribbon.

\subsection{Poetic Symmetry}

For classical Persian couplets (e.g., first two lines of the \textit{Shahnameh}), the two lines exhibit dihedral symmetry \(D_2\):
\[
\text{Line}_2 = \text{Line}_1 \circ \text{reflection}
\]
verified by identical start/end vowels and mirrored stress positions.

\subsection{Fractal Dimension}

The fractal dimension of the vowel path (including edges) is \(D=1\) (ordinary curve). The vertex set alone has coarse‑scale dimension \(\approx 0.26\) due to clustering into 6 points.

