\section{Universal Reduction Template}

For an arbitrary language \(L\), the reduction follows the same ten steps:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Phoneme inventory} (finite set \(\mathcal{P}\)).
    \item \textbf{Feature space} \(\R^n\) where \(n\) = number of distinctive features (e.g., height, backness, roundness, tone, length, nasality, voicing, etc.). Embed each phoneme into \(\R^n\).
    \item \textbf{Phonological monoid} \(\mathcal{P}^*\) possibly modulo phonotactic relations (presented monoid).
    \item \textbf{Stress function} \(\sigma : \mathcal{P}^* \to \N\) (lexical or predictable).
    \item \textbf{Reduction rules} \(\rho : \mathcal{P}^* \to \mathcal{P}^*\) (context‑dependent neutralization).
    \item \textbf{Grammar} \(G\): a finite set of production rules (regular, context‑free, or context‑sensitive) with \(\lambda\)-semantics.
    \item \textbf{Prosody function} \(\pitch: \text{ClauseType}\times\R\to\R\) (piecewise linear).
    \item \textbf{Visual form}: the time‑ordered path \(\gamma\) in \(\R^n\) with stress highlighting and reduction fading, overlaid with the syntax tree.
    \item \textbf{Symmetries}: group actions (harmonic, poetic, morphological).
    \item \textbf{Computational implementation} in a typed functional language.
\end{enumerate}

This template applies to every natural language. The concrete numbers (dimension \(n\), specific feature vectors, stress rule, grammar rules) are language‑specific, but the form is universal.

\section{The Ribbon Representation Theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Ribbon Representation Theorem]
For any natural language \(L\) and any utterance \(U\) of \(L\), there exists:
\begin{itemize}
    \item a finite‑dimensional real vector space \(\F \cong \R^n\) (feature space),
    \item a finite set \(\mathcal{P}\subset\F\) of phoneme vectors,
    \item a continuous, piecewise linear path \(\gamma:[0,T]\to\F\) (the ribbon),
    \item a rooted tree \(\mathcal{T}\) (the syntax tree),
    \item a continuous function \(\pi:[0,T]\to\R\) (the prosody),
\end{itemize}
such that:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \(\gamma(t)\) visits the points of \(\mathcal{P}\) in the temporal order of the phonemes of \(U\);
    \item the leaves of \(\mathcal{T}\) correspond to the words of \(U\) and are attached to \(\gamma\) on the time intervals of those words;
    \item \(\pi\) is piecewise linear between stress peaks and represents the pitch contour;
    \item the triple \((\gamma,\mathcal{T},\pi)\) is unique up to orientation‑preserving reparametrization of time.
\end{enumerate}
Conversely, every such triple satisfying the constraints of a language \(L\) corresponds to a well‑formed utterance of \(L\).
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
We construct the objects explicitly.

\noindent\textbf{Step 1 – Feature space.}
Let \(\mathcal{D}\) be the set of all distinctive features recognized by the International Phonetic Association. It is finite, say \(|\mathcal{D}| = N\). Define \(\F = \R^N\) and for each phoneme \(p\) of \(L\) assign a vector \(v(p) \in \R^N\) whose coordinates are the values of those features (binary or continuous). This gives the finite set \(\mathcal{P} = \{v(p) \mid p \text{ is a phoneme of } L\}\).

\noindent\textbf{Step 2 – Ribbon \(\gamma\).}
Let the utterance \(U\) be a sequence of phonemes \(p_1 p_2 \dots p_k\) with real durations. Let \(t_0=0, t_1, \dots, t_k = T\) be the cumulative times. Define \(\gamma\) on \([t_{j-1}, t_j]\) as the linear interpolation between \(v(p_{j-1})\) and \(v(p_j)\):
\[
\gamma(t) = v(p_{j-1}) + \frac{t-t_{j-1}}{t_j-t_{j-1}}\bigl(v(p_j)-v(p_{j-1})\bigr).
\]
Thus \(\gamma\) is continuous, piecewise linear, and visits each phoneme vector in temporal order.

\noindent\textbf{Step 3 – Syntax tree \(\mathcal{T}\).}
The utterance has a grammatical parse tree whose leaves are the words. Each word \(w_i\) spans a contiguous subinterval of time. Attach the leaf corresponding to \(w_i\) to the curve \(\gamma\) at the point \(\gamma(t)\) for any \(t\) inside that interval (e.g., the midpoint). The tree structure is a rooted, labeled, ordered tree.

\noindent\textbf{Step 4 – Prosody \(\pi\).}
The pitch contour of the utterance is a continuous function of time. Between perceived stress peaks (which occur at times \(\tau_1<\dots<\tau_m\)), the pitch changes with a constant slope (negative for declarative, positive for questions, etc.). Hence \(\pi\) is piecewise linear. We can write
\[
\pi(t) = \pi(0) + \sum_{r=1}^{m} c_r \cdot (t - \tau_r) \cdot \mathbf{1}_{[\tau_r,\tau_{r+1}]}(t)
\]
with \(\tau_{m+1}=T\) and appropriate slopes \(c_r\).

\noindent\textbf{Step 5 – Uniqueness and universality.}
The construction uses only the finite data of the language (phoneme set, grammar, stress rules) and the actual timing of the utterance. Different utterances yield different triples. Conversely, given a triple \((\gamma,\mathcal{T},\pi)\) that respects the language’s phonotactic and grammatical constraints, we can read off the phoneme sequence from the order of visited points in \(\mathcal{P}\), recover words from the leaf attachments, and reconstruct the pitch. Hence the mapping is a bijection between well‑formed utterances and admissible triples.

Thus every utterance of every language is isomorphic to a ribbon carrying a tree and a wave.
\end{proof}

\noindent\textbf{Q.E.D.}

