\section{Conclusion}

We have shown that every natural language admits a complete mathematical reduction to a finite set of algebraic, geometric, and computational objects. The centerpiece is the \textbf{Ribbon Representation Theorem}, which states that every utterance is isomorphic to a piecewise linear path in a finite‑dimensional feature space, carrying a syntax tree and a pitch contour. This theorem is universal, constructive, and ends any search for a deeper structure: the ribbon, the tree, and the wave are the final mathematical essence of language.

